From 4394e6a2efdc8a15d8586f9da70e9839db8cf619 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefano Bocconi <Stefano.Bocconi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 12:41:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Escape dots and backslashes inside labels in names

A label may hold any octet (RFC 2181 section 11), a dot included: RFC 6763
section 4.1.1 allows one in a DNS-SD instance name such as "Printer v2.1".
unpack.Name copies such a label into the name string as is, so
"Printer v2.1._ipp._tcp.example." reads as the two labels "Printer v2" and
"1", and pack.Name sends it on as two: a name that passes through the library
comes out as a different name. Writing "\." does not help either, as
pack.Name splits at every dot and keeps the backslash as an octet of the label
before it. A proxy that unpacks a request and packs its names again (for
instance an RFC 9665 SRP registrar forwarding a registration) therefore
registers a name the client never asked for.

Use the RFC 1035 section 5.1 escapes in names, as github.com/miekg/dns (v1)
does in UnpackDomainName, PackDomainName, SplitDomainName and IsFqdn, and as
this library already does for the mailbox names of SOA, RP, MINFO, MB, MG and
MR (unpack.MName, pack.MName):

- unpack.Name escapes a dot or a backslash inside a label (\. and \\), the two
  escapes RFC 6763 section 4.3 recommends. Every other octet is still copied
  as is, so UTF-8 names stay readable, unlike v1's \DDD.
- pack.Name ends a label only at an unescaped dot, and reads \X as the octet X
  and \DDD as the octet with decimal value DDD. The 63-octet label limit
  counts unescaped octets.
- dnsutil.Next, Prev, IsFqdn, IsName and Split skip escaped dots; the
  zdnsutil.go copies are regenerated with "go run dnsutil_generate.go".

A name with no dot or backslash inside a label is unchanged, on the wire and
as a string. A name string holding a backslash changes meaning: "a\.b." was
the labels "a\" and "b" and is now the one label "a.b". So TestIsFqdn's
"miek.nl\." is no longer fully qualified, as in v1 (TestIsFqdnEscaped).

Not changed: MName, which escapes dots but not backslashes, and compareLabel,
which for a label holding escapes orders it by its escaped form rather than
by its octets (RFC 4034 section 6.1).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---

Notes (not part of the commit message; git am drops them):

Base: codeberg.org/miekg/dns v0.6.117, the latest release when this was
written. It does not apply to v0.6.82 (internal/pack/pack.go has changed
since). Apply in a checkout of that release with "git am" or "patch -p1".

Tested with Go 1.27.0: "go vet" is clean and "go test ./..." passes in all 37
packages with tests. The new tests (TestMsgEscapedNames, TestNameEscapes, the
new unpack.TestName cases, and the new dnsutil cases) all fail without the
source changes.

Reference behavior, github.com/miekg/dns v1.1.73:
  msg.go     UnpackDomainName  escapes . \ and other special octets
             (isDomainNameLabelSpecial in types.go), \DDD for non-printable
  msg.go     PackDomainName    honors \X and \DDD
  labels.go  SplitDomainName   splits at unescaped dots only
  defaults.go IsFqdn           false for a name ending in an escaped dot
                               (labels_test.go TestIsFqdnEscaped)
A wire label "Printer v2.1" unpacks with v1 to "Printer\ v2\.1._ipp._tcp.example."
and packs back to identical bytes; with v0.6.117 unmodified it packs back as
the two labels "Printer v2" and "1".

